-		<English>[TAB]The term public school has different (and in some cases contradictory) meanings due to regional differences. In most of the world, it refers to a government-funded primary or secondary school; in some countries, it can refer to government-funded higher education; in England, Wales and Northern Ireland it refers to certain elite, private primary and secondary schools. [PARAGRAPH:2]In many English-speaking nations (but only in Scotland within the United Kingdom), a public school is a school that is financed and run by the government and does not charge tuition fees in order for children to attend. This is in contrast to a private school (also known as an "independent school"). Here, the word "public" is used in the sense of "public library", i.e. provided to the public at public expense. These public schools range in classes from kindergarten to four years of high school or secondary school, normally lasting until one is 17/18 years of age. [PARAGRAPH:2]There are variations on the name, such as "state school", which is used in the UK and some other countries. In Hong Kong they are called "government schools".</English>
